    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Seeing hail-bruised shingles or a stained ceiling and then receiving a letter that says “no coverage” is maddening. If you are facing a <strong>denied insurance claim in Dallas</strong>, the most productive move is to figure out exactly why the insurer reached that decision and whether the evidence supports it. Some denials are correct under the policy. Others come from a quick inspection after a busy storm, a missing document, or a cause-of-loss opinion that does not hold up under a closer look. Dallas Public Adjuster reviews denials for homeowners and business owners across Dallas County and helps them decide on a realistic next step.</p>

<h2>Why Property Claims Get Denied in Dallas</h2>
<p>Dallas County sees a lot of severe weather. NOAA has logged 841 hail reports in the county since 1955, and 250 of them were golf-ball size or larger. In heavy years, such as 2011, 2012, and 2023, insurers can receive a large volume of claims from the same storms in a matter of days. When outside adjusters are brought in to handle the volume, inspections can be short, and damage on steep Park Cities roofs, low-slope commercial membranes, or metal accessories is easy to overlook.</p>
<p>Common denial themes we see include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Wear and tear or age.</strong> Older composition roofs are often blamed on granule loss or deterioration rather than hail.</li>
<li><strong>Cosmetic damage only.</strong> Dents in metal roofing or trim may be excluded under some policies’ cosmetic-damage provisions.</li>
<li><strong>No storm-created opening.</strong> Interior water damage is denied because the insurer found no wind or hail opening in the roof.</li>
<li><strong>Long-term leak or seepage.</strong> Plumbing claims are denied as gradual rather than sudden. See <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Flood exclusion.</strong> Water damage after heavy rain is attributed to rising surface water. See <a href="/services/flood-damage-claims/">flood damage claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Foundation movement.</strong> Cracks are blamed on expansive clay soils rather than a covered event.</li>
<li><strong>Below the deductible.</strong> The insurer’s estimate comes in under a percentage wind/hail deductible.</li>
<li><strong>Late notice.</strong> The insurer says reporting delays prevented a fair investigation.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Reading the Denial Letter Line by Line</h2>
<p>Do not read the denial letter on its own. Put it next to your full policy, including the declarations page and every endorsement, and look for:</p>
<ol>
<li>The exact exclusion, condition, or definition the insurer quoted.</li>
<li>Whether the decision is a full denial or a partial one.</li>
<li>Who inspected and when: a field adjuster, a ladder-assist inspector, or an engineer.</li>
<li>References to photos, reports, or weather data you have not seen.</li>
<li>Any deadline for responding or providing more information.</li>
</ol>
<p>If the reasoning is vague, ask the insurer in writing to identify the policy language and the facts it relied on. A specific answer is much easier to evaluate.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/What-to-Do-If-Your-Hail-Claim-Was-Denied-in-Texas-4-1024x683.webp" alt="Two men at a desk reviewing printed roof damage photos with roof images on a monitor" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Comparing the insurer’s photos with an independent inspection often shows what the first visit missed.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Evidence That May Support a Second Look</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Denial reason</th><th>Evidence that may be relevant</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Age or wear and tear</td><td>Roof age and maintenance records, close-up photos of fresh fractures, test squares, comparison with undamaged slopes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pre-existing damage</td><td>NOAA storm reports near your address, pre-loss photos, home purchase inspection, prior claim history</td></tr>
<tr><td>No storm-created opening</td><td>Roof photos directly above the interior stain, damaged flashing or vents, moisture readings</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gradual leak</td><td>Plumber’s report on the failed part, the date the leak was found, mitigation logs</td></tr>
<tr><td>Below deductible</td><td>A full, independent line-item estimate that captures all covered damage</td></tr>
<tr><td>Late notice</td><td>When the damage was discovered, why it was not visible earlier, and all communication records</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Weather records can tie damage to a specific date. Our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup</a> tool and <a href="/storm-history/">Dallas storm history</a> page draw on NOAA records for Dallas County.</p>

