<h2>Options After a Denial in Texas</h2>
<h3>Request a re-inspection</h3>
<p>Insurers will often re-inspect when presented with new evidence. Being there, with your own documentation and a clear list of the damage you want examined, can make the second visit more thorough.</p>
<h3>Submit additional documentation</h3>
<p>A written response with photos, an independent estimate, and weather data gives the insurer something concrete to reconsider. Keep copies and send it in a way you can track.</p>
<h3>Consider appraisal for partial denials</h3>
<p>If the insurer agrees some damage is covered but disputes the amount, <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">insurance appraisal</a> may be available under your policy. Appraisal does not usually decide whether a loss is covered.</p>
<h3>File a complaint with TDI</h3>
<p>If you believe your claim was handled improperly, you can file a complaint with the <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Department of Insurance</a>. A complaint does not decide coverage, but it creates a record with the state regulator.</p>
<h3>Talk to an attorney when needed</h3>
<p>Public adjusters are not attorneys. If the insurer alleges fraud, if you believe the denial was in bad faith, or if a policy deadline to sue is near, consult a Texas attorney promptly.</p>

<h2>How a Public Adjuster in Dallas Can Help</h2>
<p>We start by reading the denial and your policy together and telling you honestly whether the decision looks supportable. If it does not, we can re-inspect the property, document the damage with photos and Matterport 3D imaging, prepare an Xactimate estimate, and present a written response to the insurer. If part of the claim was accepted but paid low, we can also address the <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid portion</a>. Joseph Dittman’s background includes carrier-side adjusting, which helps in understanding how denials are written and reviewed.</p>
<p>We cannot guarantee a reversal. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. For hail-specific guidance, read <a href="/what-to-do-if-your-hail-claim-was-denied-in-texas/">what to do if your hail claim was denied in Texas</a>, or request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a>.</p>
